¶
'''waggie''' (wag′i), ''n.'' [< ''wag''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ie'', ''-y''<sup>2</sup>.] The wagtail, a bird. [Prov. Eng.]

'''wagging''' (wag′ing), ''n.'' [< ME. ''waggynge''; verbal n. of ''wag''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] A stirring; moving; waving; oscillation; vibration.
{{smaller block|The folk devyne at ''waggynge'' of a stre.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 1745.}}
A wanton ''wagging'' of your head, thus (a feather will teach you).
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Cynthia’s Revels, ii. 1.}}}}

'''waggish''' (wag′ish), ''a.'' [< ''wag''<sup>2</sup> + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Like a wag; abounding in sportive or jocular tricks, antics, sayings, etc.; roguish in merriment or good humor; frolicsome.
{{smaller block|Jack, thou think’st thyself in the Forecastle, thou’rt so ''waggish''.
{{right|''Wycherley'', Plain Dealer, i. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' Done, concocted, or manifested in waggery or sport: as, a ''waggish'' trick; “''waggish'' good humor,” ''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 431. ='''Syn.''' {{smaller|Jocular, jocose, humorous, sportive, facetious, droll.}}

'''waggishly''' (wag′ish-li), ''adv.'' [< ''waggish'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a waggish manner; in sport.
{{smaller block|Let’s wanton it a little, and talk ''waggishly''.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Epicœne, v. 1.}}}}

'''waggishness''' (wag′ish-nes), ''n.'' [< ''waggish'' + ''-ness''.] The state or character of being waggish; mischievous sport; wanton merriment; jocularity; also, a joke or trick.
{{smaller block|Busbechius reporteth a Christian boy in Constantinople had like to have been stoned for gagging in a ''waggishness'' a long-billed fowl.
{{right|''Bacon'', Goodness, and Goodness of Nature (ed. 1887).}}}}

